<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{.language}}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{.title}}</title>
<meta name="description" content="{{.description}}">
<link rel="canonical" href="{{.url}}">
<meta property="og:type" content="website">
<meta property="og:url" content="{{.url}}">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{.title}}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{{.description}}">
<meta property="og:image" content="{{.socialImage.src}}">
<meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{.socialImage.alt}}">
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
<link rel="preload" href="assets/fonts/tektur-var.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
<link rel="preload" href="assets/fonts/martian-mono-var.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
<meta name="theme-color" content="#04070b">
<script>document.documentElement.classList.add("js")</script>
{{style "assets/styles/main.css"}}
</head>
<body>
<header class="top">
  <a class="brand" href="{{.urls.home}}">GhostPaw<span class="crumb">/affinity</span></a>
  <nav aria-label="affinity elsewhere">
    <a href="{{.urls.demo}}">Live demo</a>
    <a href="{{.urls.github}}">GitHub</a>
    <a href="{{.urls.npm}}">npm</a>
  </nav>
</header>
<main>
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="kicker"><code>@ghostpaw/affinity</code> <span class="ver">0.5.0</span></p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">Who have you been meaning to call?</h1>
      <p class="lede">affinity keeps the people your agent deals with, the promises made to them and the dates that matter in your SQLite file. It works out who is drifting from what happened, so nobody has to keep a list of who to call.</p>
      <div class="install">
        <p class="cmd-label" id="bin-label">Install the binary, no runtime needed</p>
        <div class="os-pick" role="radiogroup" aria-labelledby="bin-label">
          {{range $i, $t := .data.install.targets}}<input type="radio" name="os" id="os-{{$t.id}}" value="{{$t.id}}"{{if eq $i 0}} checked{{end}}><label for="os-{{$t.id}}">{{$t.label}}</label>{{end}}
          {{range .data.install.targets}}<div class="cmd os-cmd" data-os="{{.id}}" data-shell="{{.shell}}"><input id="hero-{{.id}}" readonly value="{{.cmd}}" aria-label="Install command for {{.label}}" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="hero-{{.id}}">Copy</button></div>{{end}}
        </div>
        <p class="cmd-note">Downloads the latest release, checks it against SHA256SUMS and puts <code>ghostpaw-affinity</code> on your PATH. Then: <code>ghostpaw-affinity mcp</code> for MCP, <code>ghostpaw-affinity skill</code> for the cheat sheet.</p>
        <p class="cmd-label" id="mcp-label">npx / npm, for npm-based environments</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="cmd-mcp" readonly value="npx -y @ghostpaw/affinity mcp" aria-labelledby="mcp-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="cmd-mcp">Copy</button></div>
        <p class="cmd-label" id="lib-label">Library, for harnesses</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="cmd-lib" readonly value="npm install @ghostpaw/affinity" aria-labelledby="lib-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="cmd-lib">Copy</button></div>
      </div>
      <p class="actions"><a class="button" href="#run">Replay the run</a> <a href="{{.urls.github}}">Read the source on GitHub</a></p>
      <p class="terms">Free and MIT licensed. The binary needs no runtime; the npm package needs Node.js 24 or newer and has zero runtime dependencies. Everything stays in <code>~/.ghostpaw/affinity.db</code> on your machine.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="hero-art frame">
      <div class="scope"><picture><source media="(min-width: 52rem)" srcset="assets/images/hero-720.webp" width="720" height="540"><img src="assets/images/hero-480.webp" width="480" height="360" alt="A radar scope drawn in thin cyan light with a sweeping wedge, a ghost paw print at its centre and three points, the farthest one violet and drifting outward." fetchpriority="high"></picture><span class="scan" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
      <p class="readout" aria-label="Zero runtime dependencies"><span class="big">0</span><span>runtime dependencies</span></p>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="run" class="sec run-sec" aria-labelledby="run-title">
    <h2 id="run-title">Three people, five calls</h2>
    <p class="intro">Jun is at the top of the radar. One evening out moves him to the bottom.</p>
    <p class="intro">This is a replay of a real run. We made these calls against affinity 0.3.0 on 2026-10-24 with a fixed clock, and every number below is what came back. Mika knows three people: Ren, an old roommate she called six days ago; Jun, a climbing partner she last saw 27 days ago; and Ada, who she met at a conference 41 days ago. Run the steps in order.</p>
    <div class="run">
      <div class="state" role="region" aria-labelledby="state-title" aria-live="polite">
        <p class="state-label" id="state-title">Radar and open items</p>
        <ul id="state-list">
          <li class="t-violet"><span class="k">Jun</span><span class="v">0.347 · drifting</span></li>
          <li><span class="k">Ada</span><span class="v">0.124 · routine</span></li>
          <li><span class="k">Ren</span><span class="v">0.000 · routine</span></li>
          <li><span class="k">Open promises</span><span class="v">none</span></li>
          <li><span class="k">Next 60 days</span><span class="v">nothing</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="state-foot" id="state-foot">read.listRadar(db), highest drift first</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li class="step" id="step-1">
          <h3><span class="n">01</span> An evening out with Jun</h3>
          <p>Mika records what happened. She does not set a score.</p>
          <pre class="call"><code>write.recordInteraction(db, {
  type: 'activity',
  occurredAt: now,
  summary: 'Climbing with Jun, then dinner',
  significance: 6,
  participants: [
    { contactId: mika.id, role: 'actor', directionality: 'mutual' },
    { contactId: jun.id, role: 'recipient', directionality: 'mutual' },
  ],
});</code></pre>
          <div class="result"><pre><code>// derivedEffects
[{ affinityDelta: 0.245, trustDelta: 0.025, rankAfter: 2, cadenceAfter: 17 }]

read.listRadar(db).map((r) =&gt; r.name)
// ['Ada', 'Ren', 'Jun']</code></pre><p class="say">Jun goes from the top of the radar to the bottom. The library also stretched his expected cadence from 14 to 17 days.</p></div>
          <button type="button" class="act run-btn">Run step 1</button>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="step-2">
          <h3><span class="n">02</span> A promise to Ren</h3>
          <p>A commitment is a journal entry with a resolution that starts out open.</p>
          <pre class="call"><code>const promise = write.recordCommitment(db, {
  commitmentType: 'promise',
  occurredAt: now,
  summary: 'Send Ren the notes on the offer letter',
  significance: 5,
  participants: [
    { contactId: mika.id, role: 'actor', directionality: 'owner_initiated' },
    { contactId: ren.id, role: 'recipient' },
  ],
});</code></pre>
          <div class="result"><pre><code>// derivedEffects
[{ affinityDelta: 0.031, trustDelta: -0.044, rankAfter: 3, cadenceAfter: 12 }]

read.listOpenCommitments(db).length
// 1</code></pre><p class="say">Ren's trust went down by 0.044 when the promise was recorded. It now shows as open.</p></div>
          <button type="button" class="act run-btn">Run step 2</button>
        </li>
        <li class="step refused" id="step-3">
          <h3><span class="n">03</span> Close it as “done”</h3>
          <p>This is how an agent would naturally put it.</p>
          <pre class="call"><code>write.resolveCommitment(db, promise.primary.id, 'done');</code></pre>
          <div class="result"><pre><code>// AffinityValidationError
// resolution must be kept, cancelled, or broken</code></pre><p class="say">Refused. A promise is kept, cancelled or broken, and “done” is none of those.</p></div>
          <button type="button" class="act run-btn">Run step 3</button>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="step-4">
          <h3><span class="n">04</span> Close it as kept</h3>
          <pre class="call"><code>write.resolveCommitment(db, promise.primary.id, 'kept');</code></pre>
          <div class="result"><pre><code>// derivedEffects
[]

read.listOpenCommitments(db).length
// 0</code></pre><p class="say">The promise is closed. Keeping it did not add anything back in this run: Ren's trust stays at 0.669.</p></div>
          <button type="button" class="act run-btn">Run step 4</button>
        </li>
        <li class="step" id="step-5">
          <h3><span class="n">05</span> Jun's birthday</h3>
          <p>A date anchor is a yearly date on a contact.</p>
          <pre class="call"><code>write.addDateAnchor(db, {
  target: { kind: 'contact', contactId: jun.id },
  recurrenceKind: 'birthday',
  anchorMonth: 11,
  anchorDay: 12,
  summary: "Jun's birthday",
  significance: 7,
});</code></pre>
          <div class="result"><pre><code>read.listUpcomingDates(db, { horizonDays: 60 })
// [{ summary: "Jun's birthday", recurrenceKind: 'birthday', occursOn: '2026-11-12' }]</code></pre><p class="say">It turns up 19 days ahead, with no calendar entry needed.</p></div>
          <button type="button" class="act run-btn">Run step 5</button>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="run-tools"><button type="button" class="button ghost" id="reset">Start over</button><p id="run-status" role="status"></p></div>
    <p class="note">The radar is refreshed when an event is written, not when you read it. That is why this replay never moves the clock: without a new write, the order stays where the last write left it.</p>
    <p class="note">Every write returns <code>derivedEffects</code>, so your agent can say what changed instead of guessing.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="sec explain" aria-labelledby="explain-title">
    <div class="sec-head">
    <h2 id="explain-title">What it keeps, and what it works out</h2>
    <img class="graph" src="assets/images/art-640.webp" width="640" height="360" alt="Hexagon nodes joined by threads of cyan light of different weights, a bright node at the centre and one thin violet thread fading out." loading="lazy">
    </div>
    <p class="intro">A relationship is its own record, not a field on a contact card.</p>
    <dl class="form">
      <div><dt>Contact</dt><dd>A person, company, team, service, pet, household or group, plus one owner contact for you.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Identity</dt><dd>An email, phone number, URL or account id that points at exactly one contact. Duplicates can be merged or dismissed.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Social link</dt><dd>The relationship itself, with rank, affinity, trust, cadence, a short bond note and a state.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tie</dt><dd>A structural fact such as <code>works_at</code>, <code>reports_to</code> or kinship.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Journal entry</dt><dd>What happened: an interaction, observation, milestone, transaction or commitment.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Date anchor</dt><dd>A yearly date such as a birthday, anniversary or renewal.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Attribute</dt><dd>Free keys and values for preferences, on contacts and on links.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="note">Rank, trust, drift and the rest are computed from the journal and the ties. None of them is a status flag your code sets by hand.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="sec worry" aria-labelledby="worry-title">
    <h2 id="worry-title">If a people database makes you uneasy</h2>
    <div class="worry-grid">
      <p>The usual cost is the client or friend you forgot to follow up with. The usual fear is the opposite: an assistant that keeps a page on everyone you mention, which sounds like a dossier, and recent coverage of exactly that is fair warning.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>It is a single binary, a library and a local MCP server. There is no hosted service and nothing leaves the SQLite file unless your code sends it.</li>
        <li>It stores what you or your agent record. It does not read your mail, messages or contacts on its own.</li>
        <li>The MCP server's file is <code>~/.ghostpaw/affinity.db</code>, or wherever <code>GHOSTPAW_AFFINITY_DB</code> or <code>--db</code> points.</li>
        <li>Twelve tools with JSON Schemas, among them <code>record_event</code>, <code>manage_commitment</code> and <code>review_affinity</code>. Each returns success, no_op, needs_clarification or error.</li>
        <li>The release binary needs no runtime. The npm package needs Node.js 24 or newer, because it uses the built-in <code>node:sqlite</code>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec field" aria-labelledby="field-title">
    <h2 id="field-title">Next to what people use for this today</h2>
    <p class="intro">Most people keep relationships in tools built for something else. These are the three that come up most, and where affinity differs.</p>
    <div class="field-rows">
      <article class="field-row"><h3>Phone contacts and calendar birthdays</h3><p class="gap">They hold a number and a date. They do not hold how long it has been, or what you promised.</p><p class="here">Each relationship has a cadence, so drift is computed. A promise stays open until it is kept, cancelled or broken.</p></article>
      <article class="field-row"><h3>A spreadsheet or notes file</h3><p class="gap">Fine until the question is who you have not talked to in a while. Then someone has to sort it by hand.</p><p class="here"><code>read.listRadar</code> returns that order, recomputed on every recorded event.</p></article>
      <article class="field-row"><h3>A hosted personal CRM with an MCP endpoint</h3><p class="gap">Your agent writes about the people in your life to someone else's server, behind an OAuth sign-in.</p><p class="here">The data is a SQLite file you open. The MCP server runs on your machine.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="note">affinity has no app and no sync. It is the store your code or your agent writes into; any interface on top is yours to build.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="sec numbers" aria-labelledby="numbers-title">
    <h2 id="numbers-title">What it costs to add</h2>
    <dl class="ledger">
      <div><dt>Runtime dependencies</dt><dd>0</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Node.js</dt><dd>24 or newer (node:sqlite)</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Size of dist/index.js, gzip</dt><dd>56.1 KB</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Import time</dt><dd>about 4.8 ms</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tests</dt><dd>459 passing</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Line coverage</dt><dd>95.4%</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Agent tools</dt><dd>12</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="note">Measured 2026-10-24 on the maintainer's machine with Node.js 24.14.0 against affinity 0.3.0: size of the published dist/index.js at gzip -9, median of 11 cold imports, tests and coverage from the v0.3.0 tag. The replay above was re-run against the same release on that day and gave the same output.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="binaries" class="sec binaries" aria-labelledby="binaries-title">
    <h2 id="binaries-title">One binary per platform</h2>
    <p class="intro">Each release ships <code>ghostpaw-affinity</code> with Node.js inside: the same CLI and MCP server as npm. These commands download latest, verify SHA256SUMS and install to the listed directory; Linux adds <code>~/.local/bin</code> to PATH via <code>~/.profile</code> only if missing. To pin, replace the latest lookup with <code>V=0.6.0</code>.</p>
    <div class="bin-cmds">
        {{range .data.install.targets}}<div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-{{.id}}-label">{{.label}}</p>
        <div class="cmd" data-shell="{{.shell}}"><input id="bin-{{.id}}" readonly value="{{.cmd}}" aria-labelledby="bin-{{.id}}-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-{{.id}}">Copy</button></div></div>
        {{end}}
    </div>
    <h3 class="bin-sub">npx / npm, for npm-based environments</h3>
    <div class="bin-cmds">
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-npx-label">npx, with Node.js 24 or newer</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-npx" readonly value="npx -y @ghostpaw/affinity --help" aria-labelledby="bin-npx-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-npx">Copy</button></div></div>
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-npmg-label">Global install</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-npmg" readonly value="npm install -g @ghostpaw/affinity" aria-labelledby="bin-npmg-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-npmg">Copy</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <h3 class="bin-sub">Library, for harnesses</h3>
    <div class="bin-cmds">
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-lib-label">Add it to a Node.js 24 project</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-lib" readonly value="npm install @ghostpaw/affinity" aria-labelledby="bin-lib-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-lib">Copy</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <h3 class="bin-sub">Other installers</h3>
    <div class="bin-cmds">
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-sh-label">install.sh, macOS and Linux</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-sh" readonly value="curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GhostPawJS/affinity/main/install.sh | sh" aria-labelledby="bin-sh-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-sh">Copy</button></div></div>
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-ps-label">install.ps1, Windows PowerShell</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-ps" readonly value="irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GhostPawJS/affinity/main/install.ps1 | iex" aria-labelledby="bin-ps-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-ps">Copy</button></div></div>
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-mise-label">mise</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-mise" readonly value="mise use -g github:GhostPawJS/affinity" aria-labelledby="bin-mise-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-mise">Copy</button></div></div>
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-ubi-label">ubi</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-ubi" readonly value="ubi --project GhostPawJS/affinity --exe ghostpaw-affinity --in ~/.local/bin" aria-labelledby="bin-ubi-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-ubi">Copy</button></div></div>
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-eget-label">eget</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-eget" readonly value="eget GhostPawJS/affinity" aria-labelledby="bin-eget-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-eget">Copy</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">Archives are 38 to 47 MB; the unpacked binary is 105 to 150 MB because the runtime is inside. Linux builds need glibc 2.28 or newer; on Alpine, use npx.</p>
    <ul class="bin-trust">
      <li>Every one-liner and installer checks the archive against SHA256SUMS and stops on a mismatch. SHA256SUMS also covers server.mcpb.</li>
      <li>Each archive and SHA256SUMS carry a GitHub build attestation. Check one with <code>gh attestation verify &lt;archive&gt; -R GhostPawJS/affinity</code>.</li>
      <li>The binaries are not code signed. macOS builds carry an ad-hoc signature, so a browser download needs <code>xattr -d com.apple.quarantine ghostpaw-affinity</code>. Windows may show a SmartScreen warning for a browser download.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="shot">
      <a href="{{.urls.cliShot}}"><img src="assets/images/cli-1600.webp" srcset="assets/images/cli-800.webp 800w, assets/images/cli-1600.webp 1600w" sizes="(min-width: 76rem) 72rem, 100vw" width="1600" height="700" decoding="async" alt="Terminal session with the ghostpaw-affinity 0.5.0 binary: --version prints the variant, contact create makes the owner Max and Jana Petersen, attribute set tags Jana gastro, event record logs a lunch nine days ago, commitment record logs a promise due two days ago, search for petersen gastro finds Jana, review commitments.open flags the promise as overdue, and review links.radar puts Jana on the radar for an overdue commitment." loading="lazy"></a>
      <figcaption>A session with the linux-x64 binary against a fresh database. Open the image for full size.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="links"><a class="more" href="{{.urls.release}}">Latest release</a><a href="{{.urls.readmeInstall}}">Install notes in the README</a><a href="{{.urls.attest}}">gh attestation verify</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="sec start" aria-labelledby="start-title">
    <h2 id="start-title">Start from the README</h2>
    <pre><code>import { DatabaseSync } from 'node:sqlite';
import { initAffinityTables, read, write } from '@ghostpaw/affinity';

const db = new DatabaseSync(':memory:');
db.exec('PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON');
initAffinityTables(db);

const { primary: owner } = write.createContact(db, {
  name: 'Mika',
  kind: 'human',
  bootstrapOwner: true,
});
const { primary: ada } = write.createContact(db, { name: 'Ada Lovelace', kind: 'human' });
write.addIdentity(db, ada.id, { type: 'email', value: 'ada@example.com' });

write.recordInteraction(db, {
  type: 'conversation',
  occurredAt: Date.now(),
  summary: 'Coffee together',
  significance: 6,
  participants: [
    { contactId: owner.id, role: 'actor', directionality: 'mutual' },
    { contactId: ada.id, role: 'recipient', directionality: 'mutual' },
  ],
});

const radar = read.listRadar(db);</code></pre>
    <p>To run it as an MCP server instead, add <code>ghostpaw-affinity mcp</code> to your client, or <code>npx -y @ghostpaw/affinity mcp</code> without the binary. <code>ghostpaw-affinity skill</code> prints the cheat sheet and the Agent Skill; <code>ghostpaw-affinity skill --list</code> lists the workflow guides.</p>
    <p class="links"><a class="more" href="{{.urls.readme}}">Read the README</a><a href="{{.urls.demo}}">Open the live demo</a><a href="{{.urls.npm}}">affinity on npm</a></p>
    <nav class="docs-map" aria-labelledby="docs-map-title">
      <h3 id="docs-map-title">{{.data.docs.heading}}</h3>
      <ul>
        {{range .data.docs.pages}}<li><a href="{{index $.urls .key}}">{{.label}}</a><span>{{.note}}</span></li>{{end}}
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </section>
</main>
